window.docContents[10].resolve({"/Airy-and-Related-Functions/Chapter-contents/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Airy-and-Related-Functions--Chapter-contents--Airy-functions":{"contents":"* 9.1 Special Notation* 9.2 Differential Equation* 9.3 Graphics* 9.4 Maclaurin Series* 9.5 Integral Representations* 9.6 Relations to Other Functions* 9.7 Asymptotic Expansions* 9.8 Modulus and Phase* 9.9 Zeros* 9.10 Integrals* 9.11 Products\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Airy and Related Functions\u0009Chapter contents","header":"Airy functions","id":"/Airy-and-Related-Functions/Chapter-contents/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Airy-and-Related-Functions--Chapter-contents--Airy-functions"},"/Elementary-Functions/___4___9-Continued-Fractions/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___9-Continued-Fractions--___4___9-Continued-Fractions":{"contents":"Section 4.9 gives continued fractions for the principal logarithm and exponential.\nThe finite approximants below are indexed by their depth: depth d retains the first\nd+1 numerators and denominators, and the displayed infinite fraction is interpreted\nas the limit as d → ∞.\n\n\n\n\\log(1+z)=\\cfrac{z}{1+\\cfrac{z}{2+\\cfrac{z}{3+\\cfrac{4z}{4+\\cfrac{4z}{5+\\cfrac{9z}{6+\\cdots}}}}}}.\nThe numerator sequence is z,z,z,4z,4z,9z,9z,…, while the denominators are\n1,2,3,4,…. Here log is the principal logarithm. The source condition\n|ph(1+z)| < π selects its branch; the explicit nonzero hypothesis below makes the\nargument of the logarithm visible in the formal statement.\n\n\\log\\!\\left(\\frac{1+z}{1-z}\\right)=\\cfrac{2z}{1-\\cfrac{z^2}{3-\\cfrac{4z^2}{5-\\cfrac{9z^2}{7-\\cdots}}}}.\nThe denominator sequence is 1,3,5,7,…; the negative signs belong to the successive\nsquare numerators. The principal branches are used away from the real cuts\n(-∞,-1] and [1,∞), which is expressed below by the corresponding condition on z.\n\ne^z=\\cfrac{1}{1-\\cfrac{z}{1+\\cfrac{z}{2-\\cfrac{z}{3+\\cfrac{z}{2-\\cdots}}}}}.\ne^z=1+\\cfrac{z}{1-\\cfrac{z}{2+\\cfrac{z}{3-\\cfrac{z}{2+\\cdots}}}}.\ne^z=1+\\cfrac{z}{1-z/2+\\cfrac{z^2/(4\\cdot3)}{1+\\cfrac{z^2/(4\\cdot15)}{1+\\cdots}}}.\nDLMF records all three forms for complex z. In the first fraction the initial\ndenominators are 1,1,2,3,2,5,2,…; in the second they are\n1,2,3,2,5,2,…. Their numerator signs alternate as displayed. In the third form\nthe first denominator is 1-z/2, every later denominator is 1, and the successive\nnoninitial numerators are z²/(4(4n²−1)).\n\ne^z-e_{n-1}(z)=\\frac{z^n}{n!}-\\cfrac{n!z}{n+1+\\cfrac{z}{n+2-\\cfrac{(n+1)z}{n+3+\\cfrac{2z}{n+4-\\cfrac{(n+2)z}{n+5+\\cdots}}}}}.\nWrite e_n(z) = ∑_{k=0}^n z^k/k!. For a positive integer n, the alternating\nsigns and the coefficients in the numerator sequence are part of the displayed\ncontinued fraction. The formal statement represents this positive integer by\nn : ℕ together with 1 ≤ n.\n\ne_n(z)=\\sum_{k=0}^{n}\\frac{z^k}{k!}.\nThe finite sum uses exactly the indices from 0 through n; in the formal\nexpression this is Finset.range (n + 1).\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.9 Continued Fractions","header":"§4.9 Continued Fractions","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___9-Continued-Fractions/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___9-Continued-Fractions--___4___9-Continued-Fractions"},"/Gamma-Function/___5___7-Series-Expansions/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Gamma-Function--___5___7-Series-Expansions--___5___7_LPAR_ii_RPAR_-Other-Series":{"contents":"When z\\ne0,-1,-2,\\ldots,\n\\psi(z)=-\\gamma-\\frac1z+\n\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{z}{k(k+z)}\n=-\\gamma+\n\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\left(\\frac1{k+1}-\\frac1{k+z}\\right).One numbered DLMF equation contains two convergent series. The Lean theorem therefore returns both\nHasSum statements together. The pole-avoidance hypothesis is written literally as exclusion of\nevery negative natural-number cast.\n\nUnder the same exclusion z\\ne0,-1,-2,\\ldots,\n\\psi\\!\\left(\\frac{z+1}{2}\\right)-\n\\psi\\!\\left(\\frac z2\\right)\n=2\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{(-1)^k}{k+z}.The factor 2 is included in each Lean summand, so the HasSum target is exactly the displayed\ndigamma difference.\n\nFor real y,\n\\Im\\psi(1+\\mathrm{i}y)=\n\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{y}{k^2+y^2}.This is a real series. Its sum is the imaginary part of Mathlib's complex digamma function at\n1 + i y; the shifted Lean index represents the source index beginning at k=1.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Gamma Function\u0009§5.7 Series Expansions","header":"§5.7(ii) Other Series","id":"/Gamma-Function/___5___7-Series-Expansions/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Gamma-Function--___5___7-Series-Expansions--___5___7_LPAR_ii_RPAR_-Other-Series"}});